---
/**
 * The seller's money: what is payable now, what is still on hold and why, and where it goes.
 *
 * ── Rewritten for SIMPLICITY, 2026-08-11 (owner, סשן א׳) ──
 * *"מאז שהתחלנו עם התשלומים הכל התבלגן לי ומרגיש לי שהלכנו לאיבוד. לכן מרגיש לי שגם המוכר ילך
 * לאיבוד. זה החלק הכי רגיש בפלטפורמה והוא צריך להיות מאוד פשוט למשתמש."* Four things changed and
 * each is a deletion rather than an addition, which is the point:
 *   1. **Every rule is stated once**, at the bottom, in `#pay-how` — four questions with four short
 *      answers. The subtitle, the tile hints and the per-row reasons used to each carry a copy of
 *      the hold rule, so it was restated five times on one screen and read nowhere.
 *   2. **A filled-in bank account is a closed summary with an "ערוך" button**, not six open inputs.
 *      An open form reads as unfinished work every time the tab is opened.
 *   3. **"שולם בעבר" and the payout history are gone from here** — they moved to the Reports tab as
 *      the `payouts` report, with a period picker and a CSV export (§6: *"זה מידע שהוא מרגיש יותר
 *      חשבונאי"*). This tab answers "when do I get paid"; what was transferred last March is a
 *      bookkeeping question, and it was the only thing on this screen that looked backwards.
 *   4. The footnote pointing at the Orders tab is deleted (§5) — the link in the action column of
 *      the table above it already goes there.
 *
 * **Two scopes on one screen, and each says which it is.** A payout is one bank transfer per seller
 * ACCOUNT — one subscription, one commission rate, one ח״פ, one bank account, however many stores
 * (`pricing.ts`, `seller-account.ts`). So "תשלום קרוב" and "שולם בעבר" cannot be split by shop and
 * are labelled "מכל החנויות". Everything else here — what is on hold, why, and the links to the
 * orders behind it — is the store in the switcher and nothing else (owner, 2026-08-11: *"אני לא
 * רואה סיבה להציג בחנות אחת נתונים של חנות אחרת"*).
 *
 * One account = one registered business is a DECISION, not an assumption nobody has examined: the
 * separate-business-entity model was designed, written down and then rejected on 2026-08-11 — a
 * seller with a second ח״פ opens a second seller account. `DB_MIGRATION_PLAN.md` §10 records why,
 * and it is closed rather than pending. Do not re-propose it.
 *
 * **Server-rendered, unlike Reports and Performance.** Those defer because a report is the heaviest
 * thing the dashboard can build. This is three small queries assembled by one call
 * (`payouts.ts#getSellerAccountFor`) and its numbers are the reason a seller opens the tab at all —
 * a screen about money that arrives a beat later reads as a screen that is not sure.
 *
 * **Every number here comes from `buildSellerAccount` and none is computed in this file.** That is
 * the rule `reporting-invariants.test.ts` exists for: the payout run and this screen must answer
 * from the same function, or a seller reads one figure and is paid another.
 *
 * The bank form lives HERE and not in Settings: splitting one form across two tabs — the money on
 * this screen, the account it goes to on another — costs the seller the one context in which the
 * fields make sense. It is ALSO offered, optionally and collapsed, on the store-opening card since
 * 2026-08-11; `payout-details.ts`'s header carries that amendment and what stayed fixed under it.
 * Both write through `parsePayoutDetails`, so neither can half-save a bank account.
 */
import { formatAgorot } from '../../lib/money.js';
import { MIN_PAYOUT_AGOROT, HOLD_DAYS_AFTER_DELIVERY, FALLBACK_DAYS_AFTER_PAYMENT, STATUTORY_RETURN_DAYS } from '../../lib/payout-schedule.js';
import { BUSINESS_TYPES, needsBankDetails, maskedBankLine, businessSummaryLine, type PayoutDetails } from '../../lib/payout-details.js';
import { splitHeldByBasis, payableHeadlineAgorot } from '../../lib/order-payout-line.js';
import type { SellerAccount } from '../../lib/seller-account.js';
import type { getT } from '../../i18n/index.js';

interface Props {
  account: SellerAccount;
  seller: PayoutDetails;
  /** More than one store, so the "all your stores" line is worth saying. */
  multiStore: boolean;
  /** Deep link into ONE store's Orders tab, filtered to the statuses one hold reason describes.
   *  Built by the page, because the store id and the tab are its business and not this component's.
   *  A null slug means the seller's only store — the page resolves it to the one in the switcher. */
  ordersHref: (basis: 'payment' | 'unshipped', storeSlug: string | null) => string;
  /** The shop in the switcher. Everything about ORDERS on this screen is scoped to it; the two
   *  account-wide figures are labelled as account-wide rather than silently pooled. */
  storeSlug: string | null;
  /** This shop's name, for the "waiting" heading when the seller has more than one. */
  storeName: string | null;
  d: ReturnType<typeof getT>['dashboard'];
}
const { account, seller, multiStore, ordersHref, storeSlug, storeName, d } = Astro.props;

/**
 * ── What used to be here, and why it is not ──
 *
 * A paged table of every held order: id, store, amount, release date, reason, action. It was
 * accurate and the owner rejected it (סשן א׳ §4): *"אני כביכול רואה שיש עוד רשימה של הזמנות,
 * בנוסף ללשונית הזמנות… אין סיבה ליצור עוד לשונית של ניהול הזמנות"*. Two tables of orders one tab
 * apart do not read as two views; they read as two places an order might be, and the seller has to
 * work out which one is authoritative.
 *
 * So the per-ORDER sentence moved to the order's own card in the Orders tab (`order-payout-line.ts`,
 * which both surfaces now derive from) and what is left here is the only thing this screen was ever
 * better placed to say: **how the waiting money splits by reason, and which part of it is waiting
 * on the seller.** That is a money question, it is three lines instead of a hundred rows, and it
 * links to the Orders tab for the rows rather than reprinting them.
 *
 * A consequence worth naming: nothing on this panel pages any more. `?paypage=` went with the
 * held-orders table and `?payhist=` went with the payout history (now the Reports tab's `payouts`
 * report). An old bookmark carrying either still opens the tab — an unknown query parameter is
 * ignored, not an error — it simply lands on the panel, which is where it was going.
 */
/** The split, and every figure in it, comes from `order-payout-line.ts#splitHeldByBasis` — nothing
 *  on this screen is computed in this file, which is the rule the panel's header already states and
 *  the reason `tests/reporting-invariants.test.ts` can prove these rows add up to the tile above
 *  them. */
/**
 * ── The waiting money is THIS STORE's, and only this store's (owner, 2026-08-11) ──
 *
 * *"אני לא רואה סיבה להציג בחנות אחת נתונים של חנות אחרת, לא תשלום, לא את הסיבות, אולי רק למעלה
 * ״תשלום קרוב מכל החנויות״"*. The previous version listed every shop's reasons on every shop's
 * screen, sectioned by name — accurate, and the wrong screen for it: the dashboard is a store's
 * dashboard, and the seller came here from the switcher having chosen one.
 *
 * So the split is scoped to the store in the switcher, and the two figures that CANNOT be scoped —
 * the next transfer and the lifetime of transfers, which are one bank account's facts and not one
 * shop's — are the account-wide strip above it, labelled as such. Nothing on this screen is now
 * both unlabelled and about somewhere else.
 */
const held = splitHeldByBasis(account.slices, storeSlug ?? undefined);
/** This shop's share of what is on hold. The account-wide `heldAgorot` is the sum over every shop,
 *  and printing it above a table that lists one shop's reasons is the mismatch this whole change is
 *  about — so the tile is computed from the same split the table renders. */
const heldHereAgorot = held.groups.reduce((t, g) => t + g.agorot, 0) + held.unknownAgorot;
/** THIS shop's "תשלום קרוב", by the rule in `order-payout-line.ts#payableHeadlineAgorot` — which the
 *  seller's OVERVIEW tab now asks as well, so the rule lives there rather than here. Its header
 *  carries why a multi-store seller and a single-store one are shown different quantities, and why
 *  the account-wide transfer has to appear somewhere on this screen too. */
const headlineAgorot = payableHeadlineAgorot(account.slices, storeSlug, multiStore, account.payableNowAgorot);

/**
 * The reason and the pending action, in the seller's language.
 *
 * The mapping from a hold basis to these two keys lives in `order-payout-line.ts` and is shared
 * with the order card, so what a seller reads beside an order and what they read beside its group
 * here cannot disagree. Only the lookup is local.
 *
 * **Both columns are LABELS now, not sentences (owner, סשן א׳ §1).** The reason used to spell the
 * whole hold rule out per row — "הכסף משתחרר 14 ימים אחרי המסירה, זה חלון ההחזרה של הקונה" — which
 * is the rule restated once per row and read nowhere. It says "טרם נמסר" and the rule is stated once,
 * in `#pay-how` at the bottom of this panel. The action was already a noun for the same reason.
 *
 * No `{n}` reaches these strings any more, which is why the day constants are no longer interpolated
 * here. They are still imported — `#pay-how` interpolates them, from `payout-schedule.ts`, because
 * those are owner decisions and a sentence promising 21 days while the code waits 30 is a promise we
 * break.
 */
const WHY: Record<string, string> = {
  delivery: d.payWhyDelivery,
  payment: d.payWhyPayment,
  unshipped: d.payWhyUnshipped,
};
const ACTION: Record<string, string> = {
  delivery: d.payActionNone,
  payment: d.payActionMarkDelivered,
  unshipped: d.payActionShip,
};
/** The one basis where the seller is BLOCKING their own money: no clock runs until they ship. It is
 *  the only action here that changes an outcome rather than a date, so it is the only one that
 *  takes a colour — three highlighted rows would highlight nothing. */
const BLOCKING = 'unshipped';
const TYPE_LABEL: Record<string, string> = {
  exempt: d.payBusinessExempt,
  licensed: d.payBusinessLicensed,
  company: d.payBusinessCompany,
};

const bankLine = maskedBankLine(seller);
/** The business line of the closed summary. Assembled by `payout-details.ts#businessSummaryLine`,
 *  which `scripts/dashboard/payouts.ts` also calls after a save — one composition rule, two
 *  renderers, no drift. */
const businessSummary = businessSummaryLine(seller, TYPE_LABEL, d.payBusinessMissing);
/**
 * The same predicate the tab's red dot is drawn from (`payout-details.ts#needsBankDetails`), asked
 * again here so the dot on the strip has a dot UNDER it, on the form it is actually about (owner,
 * סשן א׳ §5). A dot on a tab tells a seller that something in a screenful of tiles, two tables and
 * a six-field form needs them; it does not tell them which. The chain has to end on the thing to
 * do, or the top of it is just an alarm.
 *
 * Computed rather than passed: the panel already holds both inputs, and a `needsBank` prop would be
 * a second place for the condition to be decided from.
 */
const needsBank = needsBankDetails(seller, account.payableNowAgorot);
const TILE = 'border [border-color:var(--color-border)] rounded-[var(--radius)] p-3';
const CELL = 'py-2 px-2.5 text-[0.85rem]';
/** The one dot recipe on this panel — same 7px danger circle the tab strip and the order rows use,
 *  so a seller learns the mark once. Inline-block here rather than absolutely positioned: it sits
 *  in the flow of a heading instead of over the corner of a button. */
const DOT = 'inline-block w-[7px] h-[7px] rounded-full [background:var(--color-danger)] align-middle ms-2';
---

<div id="payouts-root">
  <div class="dash-panel-head mb-4 py-[0.6rem] border-b [border-color:var(--color-border)]">
    <h2 class="m-0 text-base font-semibold">{d.payTitle}</h2>
    {/* ONE line, and it says only when the money moves. Every rule it used to carry — the hold, the
        return window, the minimum — is in `#pay-how` at the bottom, once (owner, סשן א׳ §1). */}
    {/* No "כולל את כל החנויות שלך" here any more (owner, 2026-08-11). Standing at the top of a
        STORE's dashboard, that sentence made the whole screen read as somebody else's — and it was
        only ever true of two things on it. It moved to the one place it is actually about, the bank
        card, and the two figures that really are account-wide say so on themselves. */}
    <p class="muted text-[0.82rem] m-0 mt-1">{d.paySubtitle.replace('{day}', d.payDayName)}</p>
  </div>

  {/* Two tiles, not four. "שולם בעבר" left for the Reports tab (§6) — it was the only backward-
      looking figure on a screen about what happens next, and it is the one a bookkeeper wants with
      a period and an export rather than as a lifetime total nobody can reconcile against. */}
  <dl class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-3 mb-4">
    <div class={TILE}>
      {/* Named after the SHOP when there is more than one, because that is whose money it is. The
          real transfer covers every shop and is stated on its own line below the tiles. */}
      <dt class="text-[0.76rem] [color:var(--color-muted)] font-medium mb-1">{d.payPayableNow}{multiStore && storeName ? ` · ${storeName}` : ''}</dt>
      {/* The one figure a seller acts on, so it is the one that takes the accent. The others are
          context and stay in the ordinary text colour — three coloured numbers say nothing. */}
      {/* ── `data-skel`, and why a SERVER-rendered panel has one (owner, 2026-08-11) ──
          Nothing here waits on first paint — the figures arrive with the page, which is exactly why
          this tab has no loading screen and Performance does. There is one moment it DOES wait: a
          status change on the Orders tab moves money between held and payable, and `payouts.ts`
          re-reads the server's rendering of this panel. Until it lands, these tiles show the number
          from before the change — a stale figure on a money screen, presented with the confidence of
          a fresh one. So the shimmer stands in for the value while the refresh is in flight, on the
          same rule Performance's KPIs follow (`SkelBar.astro`: a seller cannot tell a real zero from
          "not here yet"). The attribute carries the SIZE so the swap causes no layout jump. */}
      <dd class={`m-0 text-[1.1rem] font-bold ${headlineAgorot > 0 ? '[color:var(--color-accent)]' : ''}`} id="pay-kpi-now" data-skel="w-24 h-[1.1rem]">{formatAgorot(headlineAgorot)}</dd>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.72rem] leading-snug">{d.payPayableNowHint}</p>
    </div>
    {/* THIS shop's share, like the tile beside it — both tiles are now the store in the switcher,
        and the one account-wide figure on the screen is the transfer sentence below them (owner,
        2026-08-11: *"זה לא רלוונטי מכל החנויות, רק החנות שהוא עליה"*). Printing the account-wide
        held figure above a table that lists one shop's reasons was the original mismatch. Computed
        from the same split the table renders, so the tile and the rows under it are one number. */}
    <div class={TILE}>
      <dt class="text-[0.76rem] [color:var(--color-muted)] font-medium mb-1">{d.payHeld}</dt>
      <dd class="m-0 text-[1.1rem] font-bold" data-skel="w-24 h-[1.1rem]">{formatAgorot(heldHereAgorot)}</dd>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.72rem] leading-snug">{d.payHeldHint}</p>
      {/* The tile's own way out, instead of a second explanatory sentence on it (owner, §3). A real
          `<a href="#pay-how">` so it works with no JS; `payouts.ts` intercepts it because an anchor
          jump on this site lands the target under the fixed header. */}
      <a class="inline-block mt-1 text-[0.72rem] underline underline-offset-2 [color:var(--color-muted)] hover:[color:var(--color-accent)]" href="#pay-how" id="pay-how-link">{d.payHeldMore}</a>
    </div>
    {/* Rendered only when it exists. A permanent "0 ₪ debt" tile teaches a seller to expect a debt
        column, which is the wrong thing for a screen most of them will never see one on. */}
    {account.carriedAgorot > 0 && (
      <div class={TILE}>
        <dt class="text-[0.76rem] [color:var(--color-muted)] font-medium mb-1">{d.payCarried}</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 text-[1.1rem] font-bold [color:var(--color-danger)]" data-skel="w-24 h-[1.1rem]">{formatAgorot(account.carriedAgorot)}</dd>
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.72rem]">{d.payCarriedHint}</p>
      </div>
    )}
  </dl>

  {/* The REAL transfer, once, and only for a seller who has more than one shop. The tiles above are
      this shop's; one bank transfer covers all of them, and a seller who read only the per-store
      figure would expect the wrong amount to arrive. Stated as a sentence rather than a fourth
      tile — it is context for the number above, not a number to act on separately.

      `payMinNote` went with it: it was a second standing sentence in this spot, and the minimum is
      one of the four things `#pay-how` now answers (owner, סשן א׳ §1). */}
  {multiStore && (
    <div class="m-0 mb-4 text-[0.82rem] flex flex-col gap-0.5">
      <p class="m-0">{d.payAccountTotal.replace('{amount}', formatAgorot(account.payableNowAgorot))}</p>
      {/* Its pair, added 2026-08-11 at the owner's request, and DIRECTLY under it on purpose: the
          two tiles above are this shop's "תשלום קרוב" and "ממתינים", and these are the same two
          questions asked of the whole account, in the same order. A seller can then read the column
          down or the pair across, and neither reading is a scope they have to work out.

          `account.heldAgorot` — the account-wide figure from `buildSellerAccount`, NOT the
          per-store `heldHereAgorot` the tile shows. They are the same number for a seller with one
          shop, which is exactly why this block is `multiStore`-only: two identical figures a line
          apart teach a seller that the scope labels mean nothing. */}
      <p class="m-0">{d.payAccountHeld.replace('{amount}', formatAgorot(account.heldAgorot))}</p>
    </div>
  )}

  {/* The banner. It appears on ONE condition — money is genuinely released and there is nowhere to
      send it — because a standing "complete your profile" prompt is the entry barrier this project
      refuses to build (`feedback_seller_form_burden`). Nothing here is required until it is. */}
  {needsBank && (
    <div class="card card--flat p-4 mb-4 [border-color:var(--color-warning)]" role="status" id="pay-no-bank-banner">
      <h3 class="text-[0.98rem] font-bold m-0">{d.payNoBankTitle}</h3>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.85rem]">{d.payNoBankBody}</p>
      {/* `self-start`, and it is not cosmetic: `.card` is `display:flex; flex-direction:column`
          (cards.css), so a direct child stretches to the card's whole width — which is how a
          `.btn`, an inline-flex element everywhere else on the site, came out as a full-width bar
          here and nowhere else. Every direct child of a `.card` that is not meant to fill it needs
          this.
          A button rather than an anchor: `href="#..."` scrolls the form's TOP to the viewport's
          top, where the fixed header covers it, so it landed in the middle of the fields
          (owner, 2026-08-10). The script takes them to the first EMPTY field instead, which is the
          thing they actually came to fill in. */}
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--sm mt-3 self-start" id="pay-goto-details">{d.payNoBankCta}</button>
    </div>
  )}

  <form class="card card--flat p-4 md:p-5" id="pay-details-form" data-unsaved-guard>
    <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0">
      {d.payBankTitle}
      {/* `id`, because scripts/dashboard/payouts.ts clears it the moment the form saves — the tab's
          own dot is server-rendered and would otherwise sit there until the next full load, which
          is the "I did the thing and it still says I didn't" state. */}
      {needsBank && <span id="pay-bank-dot" class={DOT} role="img" aria-label={d.payNoBankTitle}></span>}
    </h3>
    {/* The one thing on this screen that genuinely is account-wide and could be MISTAKEN for the
        store's, so it is said here and only here (owner, 2026-08-11: *"לא צריך להיות בכותרת, אלא
        ליד הפרטי בנק… וזה צריך להופיע רק אם יש לו מעל חנות אחת"*). A seller with one shop has no
        second shop for the sentence to be about, and telling them anyway invents a distinction they
        do not have. The two account-wide FIGURES carry their own scope in their own labels. */}
    {multiStore && <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.8rem]">{d.payBankAllStores}</p>}

    {/* ── The closed summary (owner, סשן א׳ §2) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        *"אם יש חשבון בנק לא צריך לשים שם את האינפוטים ככה פתוחים, הפרטים צריכים להופיע בתוך מסגרת
        קטנה סגורה, עם כפתור ״ערוך״"*. Six open inputs on a saved account read as unfinished work
        on the one screen a seller opens to reassure themselves that nothing is.

        **Both halves are SERVER-rendered with `hidden` on the one that does not apply**, never
        revealed by a script on load: a JS-gated visibility is the flash class this project already
        has a memory for (`project_injected_overlay_flash`). The server knows which state this is —
        `bankLine` is non-null exactly when all four fields make a payable account — so there is
        nothing for the client to decide before first paint.

        The account is MASKED here, as it is everywhere: `maskedBankLine` shows the last four digits
        so a seller recognises their own account and a screen-share does not carry the whole number.
        Its header carries the reasoning. */}
    <div
      id="pay-bank-summary"
      class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mt-3 p-3 border [border-color:var(--color-border)] rounded-[var(--radius)]"
      hidden={!bankLine}
    >
      <div class="min-w-0">
        <p class="muted m-0 text-[0.76rem] font-medium">{d.payBankOnFile}</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-0.5 text-[0.9rem] font-semibold"><code dir="ltr" id="pay-bank-summary-line">{bankLine}</code></p>
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.76rem]">{d.payBusinessOnFile} <span id="pay-business-summary">{businessSummary}</span></p>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm shrink-0" id="pay-bank-edit">{d.payBankEdit}</button>
    </div>

    {/* The fields. `hidden` while a saved account is being summarised — and they stay INSIDE the
        form rather than being rendered only on demand, so "ערוך" is a class toggle instead of a
        second renderer for a money form (`project_client_renderer_i18n_drift`). A hidden input is
        still submitted, which is correct here: pressing Save from the summary state would post the
        account exactly as it stands. */}
    <div id="pay-bank-fields" hidden={!!bankLine}>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.82rem]">{d.payBankHint}</p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 mt-4">
        {/* `inputmode="numeric"` + `dir="ltr"`: these are ASCII numbers on an RTL page, and left to
            inherit they render with their digits grouped the wrong way round. */}
        <label class="field">
          <span class="text-[0.85rem]">{d.payBankCode}</span>
          <input class="input" name="bankCode" id="pay-bank-code" dir="ltr" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" maxlength="2" value={seller.bankCode ?? ''} />
        </label>
        <label class="field">
          <span class="text-[0.85rem]">{d.payBankBranch}</span>
          <input class="input" name="bankBranch" id="pay-bank-branch" dir="ltr" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" maxlength="3" value={seller.bankBranch ?? ''} />
        </label>
        <label class="field">
          <span class="text-[0.85rem]">{d.payBankAccount}</span>
          <input class="input" name="bankAccount" id="pay-bank-account" dir="ltr" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" maxlength="20" value={seller.bankAccount ?? ''} />
        </label>
        <label class="field">
          <span class="text-[0.85rem]">{d.payBankHolder}</span>
          <input class="input" name="bankAccountHolder" id="pay-bank-holder" autocomplete="off" maxlength="120" value={seller.bankAccountHolder ?? ''} />
          <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.76rem]">{d.payBankHolderHint}</p>
        </label>
      </div>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-1">
        <label class="field">
          <span class="text-[0.85rem]">{d.payBusinessId}</span>
          <input class="input" name="businessId" id="pay-business-id" dir="ltr" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" maxlength="9" value={seller.businessId ?? ''} />
        </label>
        <label class="field">
          <span class="text-[0.85rem]">{d.payBusinessType}</span>
          <select class="input" name="businessType" id="pay-business-type">
            <option value="">{d.payBusinessNone}</option>
            {BUSINESS_TYPES.map((t) => (
              <option value={t} selected={seller.businessType === t}>{TYPE_LABEL[t]}</option>
            ))}
          </select>
          <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.76rem]">{d.payBusinessHint}</p>
        </label>
      </div>

      <p class="hidden text-sm [color:var(--color-danger)] mt-1 mb-3" id="pay-details-error" role="alert"></p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mt-3">
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn--sm" id="pay-details-save">{d.payDetailsSave}</button>
        {/* Rendered always, HIDDEN until there is a saved account to go back to. It has to exist in
            the markup because a seller filling this in for the first time collapses into the
            summary the moment they save, without a reload — so `payouts.ts` un-hides it there.
            Cancelling with nothing saved would close the fields over an EMPTY summary card, which
            is why it is not simply always visible. */}
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="pay-bank-cancel" hidden={!bankLine}>{d.payBankCancel}</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </form>

  {/* Three lines, for THIS shop. What the seller cannot get from the Orders tab is the SPLIT — how
      much of the waiting money is simply the buyer's return window running down, and how much is
      sitting still because nobody has posted the parcel.

      Scoped to the store in the switcher (owner, 2026-08-11). It used to list every shop's reasons
      under its own name on every shop's screen; the heading names this shop only when the seller
      has more than one, and the account-wide figures live in the strip at the top. */}
  <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0 mt-6 mb-2">
    {d.payHeldTitle}{multiStore && storeName ? ` \u00b7 ${storeName}` : ''}
  </h3>
  {held.groups.length === 0 && held.unknownOrders === 0 ? <p class="muted m-0 text-[0.85rem]">{d.payHeldEmpty}</p> : (
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-start">
        <thead>
          <tr class="[color:var(--color-muted)] text-[0.76rem]">
            <th class={`${CELL} text-start font-medium`}>{d.payColWhy}</th>
            <th class={`${CELL} text-start font-medium`}>{d.payColOrders}</th>
            <th class={`${CELL} text-start font-medium`}>{d.payColAmount}</th>
            <th class={`${CELL} text-start font-medium`}>{d.payColAction}</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {held.groups.map((g) => (
            <tr class="border-t [border-color:var(--color-border)]">
              <td class={CELL}>{WHY[g.basis]}</td>
              <td class={`${CELL} tabular-nums`}>{g.orders}</td>
              <td class={`${CELL} tabular-nums`}>{formatAgorot(g.agorot)}</td>
              {/* The action cell is a LINK for the two bases that have one, and plain text for the
                  one that does not — a seller told to ship an order needs the order, and sending
                  them to hunt for it in a filter menu is the half of the old table worth keeping.
                  `ordersHref` narrows the Orders tab to this shop AND to this reason's payout
                  status; the tab then says so, with a way back to the full list. */}
              <td class={`${CELL} ${g.basis === BLOCKING ? 'font-semibold [color:var(--color-warning)]' : '[color:var(--color-muted)]'}`}>
                {g.basis === 'delivery' ? ACTION[g.basis] : (
                  <a class="underline underline-offset-2 [color:inherit] hover:[color:var(--color-accent)]" href={ordersHref(g.basis, storeSlug)}>{ACTION[g.basis]}</a>
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          {held.unknownOrders > 0 && (
            <tr class="border-t [border-color:var(--color-border)]">
              <td class={CELL}>{d.payWhyUnknown}</td>
              <td class={`${CELL} tabular-nums`}>{held.unknownOrders}</td>
              <td class={`${CELL} tabular-nums`}>{formatAgorot(held.unknownAgorot)}</td>
              <td class={`${CELL} [color:var(--color-muted)]`}>{d.payActionNone}</td>
            </tr>
          )}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {/* The footnote that pointed at the Orders tab is deleted (owner, סשן א׳ §5: *"משפט מיותר"*).
          The action column above it is already a link into exactly the orders it describes, so the
          sentence was telling a seller where to find something they were being handed. */}
    </div>
  )}

  {/* ── The rules, once, at the end (owner, סשן א׳ §1) ────────────────────────────────────────
      Four questions in the order a seller asks them, each with one short answer. This is the ONLY
      place on the screen where the hold rule is spelled out; the subtitle, the tile hints and the
      table's reason column all used to carry a copy of it, which is what made a payments page feel
      like a document rather than a statement.

      A `<dl>` and not prose: a seller scanning for one of the four finds it by its question, which
      is not true of a paragraph that contains the same words.

      Every number is interpolated from `payout-schedule.ts`. Those are still owner decisions and
      `terms.astro` interpolates the same four constants — a page here promising 21 days while the
      terms promise 30 is the contradiction that matters most on this particular screen. */}
  <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0 mt-8 mb-2" id="pay-how">{d.payHowTitle}</h3>
  <dl class="card card--flat p-4 md:p-5 gap-3 text-[0.85rem]">
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold m-0">{d.payHowWhenQ}</dt>
      <dd class="muted m-0 mt-0.5">{d.payHowWhenA.replace('{day}', d.payDayName)}</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold m-0">{d.payHowHeldQ}</dt>
      <dd class="muted m-0 mt-0.5">{d.payHowHeldA.replace('{delivery}', String(HOLD_DAYS_AFTER_DELIVERY)).replace('{payment}', String(FALLBACK_DAYS_AFTER_PAYMENT))}</dd>
    </div>
    {/* Both the QUESTION and the answer carry the hold length, because the question the owner
        actually asked was *"למה 21 ולא 15?"* — a heading that says only "למה?" is answering a
        different one. Since 2026-08-16 the hold IS the statutory window, so the two numbers are the
        same one; the answer still names the law, because "14 because 14" explains nothing. */}
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold m-0">{d.payHowWhyQ.replace('{delivery}', String(HOLD_DAYS_AFTER_DELIVERY))}</dt>
      <dd class="muted m-0 mt-0.5">{d.payHowWhyA.replace('{delivery}', String(HOLD_DAYS_AFTER_DELIVERY)).replace('{statutory}', String(STATUTORY_RETURN_DAYS))}</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold m-0">{d.payHowMinQ}</dt>
      <dd class="muted m-0 mt-0.5">{d.payHowMinA.replace('{min}', formatAgorot(MIN_PAYOUT_AGOROT))}</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</div>
